Place Firebase `google-services.json` in this directory (`app/google-services.json`).

Download from Firebase Console → Project settings → Android app `io.github.stozo04.openloop`,  
or CLI (project `openloop-8c266`):

```bash
npx -y firebase-tools@latest apps:sdkconfig ANDROID \
  1:95815153197:android:c30254bb713d1e6ae96aa4 \
  --project openloop-8c266 --out app/google-services.json
```

Run from the repository root. The CLI writes the JSON directly to app/google-services.json.

Setup and Crashlytics triage: `ReverseCrashlytics.kt` and the `/crashlytics-triage` skill (`.claude/skills/crashlytics-triage/SKILL.md`).

This file is not used by the build; the JSON is gitignored.

Release APKs and bundles fail at preReleaseBuild if app/google-services.json is missing.
Debug builds remain available without Firebase configuration. With the file present,
the Google Services plugin validates the JSON and matching Android package, and
the Crashlytics plugin uploads the release mapping file.

Run the missing-config regression check with JAVA_HOME set to your JDK:

```bash
python scripts/verify-firebase-release-gate.py
```

Run this check alone, with no other Gradle builds running. It temporarily moves the
local config aside and restores it in a finally block. An interrupted process can
leave app/google-services.json.guard-test.tmp; restore that file before rebuilding.
